<Layout
	title="About The Harness"
	description="Why a deterministic kernel decides what your coding agent may do — and what it deliberately does not claim to protect you from."
	pubDate={new Date('June 19 2026')}
	updatedDate={new Date('August 14 2026')}
	heroImage={AboutHeroImage}
>
	<p>
		<strong>ai2rules</strong> is an open-source security project aimed at one problem
		in the era of autonomous developer tools: <em>ambient authority</em>.
	</p>

	<p>
		When you run an agent like Claude Code or Codex on your machine, it runs as you.
		It inherits your SSH keys, your production cloud credentials, and write access to
		everything you own. Nothing about the tool is malicious; the authority is simply
		yours, and it is handed over whole.
	</p>

	<p>
		The common answer is to write a better system prompt — "do not delete my files."
		Prompt injection is the standing proof that this cannot hold:
		<strong>you cannot filter danger with the thing being manipulated</strong>. A model
		asked to police its own inputs is one persuasive input away from not doing so.
	</p>

	<h3>The kernel approach</h3>

	<p>
		So the decision moves out of the model. You describe what the agent may do in an
		immutable <code>WorldManifest</code>, and a deterministic kernel decides every
		proposed tool call against it — before the host acts. A tool absent from the
		manifest is not merely refused; it is never offered. Data that arrived from
		somewhere untrusted is marked, and marked data cannot reach an
		externally-effectful action.
	</p>

	<p>
		The property that matters is that none of this is a judgement call. The same
		manifest and the same call always yield the same verdict, the rule that fired is
		named in the answer, and the whole decision is recorded and replayable. You can
		disagree with a verdict and go read exactly why it happened.
	</p>

	<h3>What is actually governed</h3>

	<p>
		Coverage is uneven, and pretending otherwise is the fastest way to lose a reader
		who checks. Hosts that expose a pre-tool hook — Claude Code, the Antigravity CLI,
		OpenCode — are governed deeply: every native tool call is decided by the kernel.
		Hosts that do not are reachable only through their MCP seam, via a gateway that
		shapes the tool surface and gates each call. Same kernel, same manifest, different
		depth. Which host lets you control what is measured, host by host, in the
		<a href="https://github.com/sv-pro/ai2rules/blob/main/docs/GOVERNABILITY-INDEX.md"
			>Agent Governability Index</a
		> — including the parameters where our own integration scores badly.
	</p>

	<h3>What it is not</h3>

	<p>
		It is a decision layer, not a sandbox. It rules on an action before it happens; it
		does not confine a process that is already running, and it cannot stop a tool that
		never asks. Command classification reads shell strings, which is a heuristic — the
		safety comes from unmatched commands falling into a fail-closed bucket, not from
		the patterns being exhaustive. If you need containment, you need containment. The
		full list of what this does not protect against, kept current rather than
		flattering, is in
		<a href="https://github.com/sv-pro/ai2rules/blob/main/SECURITY.md">SECURITY.md</a>.
	</p>

	<h3>How it is built</h3>

	<p>
		The harness governs its own development: the manifest in this repository is the one
		applied to the agents working on it, so a weakness in the kernel is a weakness the
		project runs on. That has caught real defects — a policy that two hosts silently
		disagreed about, a taint marker that stopped being written when a directory was
		read-only, an in-browser demo quietly running a two-month-old engine.
	</p>

	<p>
		Findings against our own kernel get a number, a fix, and a written record, whether
		they came from outside or from us. Every design decision, including the ones later
		reversed, is in
		<a href="https://github.com/sv-pro/ai2rules/blob/main/DECISIONS.md">DECISIONS.md</a>
		with the alternatives that were rejected and why. That trail is the point: a
		security tool you are asked to trust should be one you can audit instead.
	</p>
</Layout>
